## Contact backend and env vars

You can configure a simple serverless/contact backend one of three ways:

1. SendGrid (recommended for production) — set SENDGRID_API_KEY, SENDGRID_TO_EMAIL, SENDGRID_FROM_EMAIL in your provider's environment variables.
2. Formspree — set FORMSPREE_ENDPOINT to your Formspree endpoint (e.g. https://formspree.io/f/{id}).
3. No mailer — the function will accept submissions, log them to the server logs, and return success so visitors don't see errors (useful for development).

Files added

- netlify/functions/contact.js — Netlify Function handler for POST /api/contact (redirected by netlify.toml)
- api/contact.js — Vercel Serverless Function handler at /api/contact

How the frontend connects

- The frontend posts to /api/contact. For Vercel that maps to /api/contact. For Netlify the netlify.toml redirects /api/* to the function path so /api/contact works there too.

Environment variables

- SENDGRID_API_KEY — (optional) your SendGrid API key
- SENDGRID_TO_EMAIL — the recipient email address (where you want to receive messages)
- SENDGRID_FROM_EMAIL — the verified from address for SendGrid
- FORMSPREE_ENDPOINT — (optional) your Formspree endpoint URL, e.g. https://formspree.io/f/abcd1234

Examples (Netlify UI)

- Site settings → Build & deploy → Environment → Add the variables above.

Examples (Vercel UI)

- Project → Settings → Environment Variables → Add the variables above.

Notes

- If both SendGrid and Formspree are configured, the function will prefer SendGrid.
- The function will validate that name, email and message are present.
- If no mailer is configured the payload will be logged to the function logs (use provider logs to inspect submissions).

Security

- Do not commit API keys into the repo. Use provider environment variables or secret managers.
